# Working trees are LF on every platform, regardless of the contributor's
# core.autocrlf setting. The repo's gates compare bytes read straight off disk
# (golden-master fixtures in tests/server/fixtures, the control-byte guard in
# tests/architecture.test.ts, the media manifest hashes), so a CRLF-smudged
# checkout, which is what the Git for Windows installer default autocrlf=true
# produces, fails them wholesale even though CI is green. text=auto keeps git's
# binary detection for unattributed files; eol=lf pins the checkout ending.
# Later lines win per attribute, so the *.bat exemption below still holds.
# A checkout made before this rule landed heals by deleting the affected files
# and re-checking them out (git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f -- ; git checkout -- .),
# which discards uncommitted working tree changes, so commit or stash first.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated dense i18n resolved tables, plus the flat TranslationKey union emitted
# by the same build. These STAY committed (they are the tsc safety net + the runtime
# read source), but each is a machine-emitted artifact regenerated by
# `npm run i18n:build` / `npm run i18n:admin`. Marking them `linguist-generated`
# makes GitHub collapse them in pull-request diffs and excludes them from language
# statistics. Display hygiene only - not removal, not gitignore.
src/ui/i18n.resolved.generated/** linguist-generated
src/admin/i18n.resolved.generated/** linguist-generated
src/ui/i18n.catalog/translation_keys.generated.ts linguist-generated

# Windows batch scripts keep their CRLF endings: exempt from text conversion so an
# LF-normalized checkout (core.autocrlf=input) neither flags nor rewrites them.
*.bat -text
